1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,040
Hey guys, Andy here with CQE Academy.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:07,120
And in today's video, I want to talk about a really important topic, which is the seven QC tools.

3
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:18,160
Now, whether you just want to get better at work and use these tools in your everyday job, or you're preparing for something like the Green Belt exam or the Black Belt exam or the CQE exam, today's lecture is for you.

4
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:20,040
All right, let's hit it with the computer and get started.

5
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:22,760
All right, let's go ahead and jump in right into the agenda.

6
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:30,960
So we're going to start with a brief intro of the seven QC tools, kind of talk about all of them and how they fit into the problem-solving process or the improvement process.

7
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:32,640
And then we're going to go through each one.

8
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:40,120
We're going to start the flow chart, the check sheet, the Pareto chart, the cause and effect diagram, scatter diagram, histogram, and then the control charts.

9
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:49,360
And then along the way as we go through this, we're actually going to work a problem using all seven tools, and we're going to reduce the number of defects associated with our toaster.

10
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:50,520
All right, let's go and get started.

11
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:55,440
So the seven QC tools, I love this quote from Keoru Ishikawa who said.

12
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:03,140
As much as 95% of quality problems can be solved with seven fundamental tools, and I absolutely agree with that.

13
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:11,260
I think these tools are probably the seven most powerful tools, whether you're talking about green belt or black belt or quality engineering.

14
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:17,740
It doesn't matter; these seven tools are incredibly powerful for solving problems and making improvements.

15
00:01:18,220 --> 00:01:19,580
And this is a really important topic.

16
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:26,660
By the way, as we go through this, I'll make sure to talk about where we're at in something like the plan-do-check-act or the Deming cycle.

17
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:32,640
We're going to solve a problem with our toaster and we'll use either the DMAIC or the Plan-Do-Check-Act process to do it.

18
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:34,120
All right, let's get into it.

19
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:37,200
All right, so the very first tool is the flowchart.

20
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:44,200
And what a flowchart does is it's a visual tool that helps you depict the flow or the sequence of a process.

21
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:49,640
This could be things like the flow of information or the flow of tasks or material or people or decisions.

22
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:50,920
It doesn't matter.

23
00:01:50,960 --> 00:02:21,480
The reason that a flowchart is so incredibly valuable is it makes a really complex process simple and it promotes a common understanding of a process anytime you get more than one person in a room to talk about a process there's likely going to be disagreement about how the process works and I love using this analogy oftentimes when we sit down to analyze a process there's what management thinks is happening there's what the procedure says is happening there's what's actually happening on the production floor and then there's what could be happening.

24
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:28,140
And the beauty of a flowchart is it does just that, it gets everyone on the same page about what's actually happening.

25
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:29,980
And I love this quote from Dr.

26
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:35,940
Deming who said, "If you can't describe what you're doing as a process, you don't know what you're doing.

27
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:40,060
" And the best way to describe what you're doing is to use a flowchart.

28
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:41,780
And that's why this tool is so powerful.

29
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:52,780
If you're in the planning phase or the define phase, it's really good to use a flowchart, define your process and then use that flowchart to plan out your experiment and plan out how you're going to make an improvement.

30
00:02:53,060 --> 00:03:17,900
So let's do just that, let's say we're talking about our toaster and we want to make an improvement right and so the first thing we're going to do is we're going to start with the boundaries we want to analyze a process but we want to start with the boundaries first so we're going to go from receiving a work order to completing a work order that's the boundaries of our flow chart now I've got the team here because all of these activities all these tools are all team based so imagine you're sitting down with your team and the first thing you're going to do is brainstorm.

31
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:20,540
All of the steps in the process, right?

32
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:21,660
Talk to the experts.

33
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:22,900
How does the process work?

34
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:24,540
Use post-it notes, right?

35
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:25,740
Don't try to do this in some software.

36
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:28,380
Use post-it notes, write down all the activities.

37
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:37,220
And then once you're done brainstorming, organize those thoughts into that logical flow or that logical sequence of activities for your process.

38
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:41,540
And now that we have our process here, we're in that planning phase and we want to create a target, right?

39
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:43,420
What sort of improvement are we going to make?

40
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:46,660
And we want to reduce defects by twenty five percent.

41
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:50,540
Now, we can't make an improvement and we can't solve a problem without data.

42
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:53,900
And we know that most of our defects happen during final testing.

43
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:56,740
So now we need to collect a little bit of data.

44
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:58,700
And this is where the check sheet comes into play.

45
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:04,580
So the check sheet is a very simple tool for collecting, organizing and analyzing data.

46
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:09,100
Every problem you solve or every improvement you make should be based on data.

47
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:13,020
And the check sheet is probably the most powerful tool for collecting data.

48
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:37,440
There's something wrong with the check sheet that I'm showing you here on the screen and that problem is it doesn't have any metadata if you're collecting data and you want to make a high quality decision using that data, you also need metadata so when you're creating your check sheet don't forget to include things like who and when and where all those key elements of data integrity and data accuracy are really important for making high quality decisions.

49
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:40,820
Okay, so we've got the team together and again we did a little bit more brainstorming.

50
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:45,180
We said okay, at final testing we have eight defects that we want to collect some data on.

51
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:52,860
So we create this check sheet, we've got our metadata here, we hand this off to the team and they come back to us a week later with a bunch of data.

52
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:59,740
Now this is fantastic, we finally have some data that we can analyze and the question is which defect do we focus on?

53
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:01,420
I want to improve our target.

54
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:02,700
So we originally said.

55
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,220
We want to reduce defects by 25%.

56
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:08,640
Well, now that we have a little bit of data, we can actually create a target.

57
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:12,440
So, we have 145 defects across a whole week—that's seven days.

58
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:17,240
That means we're averaging about 20 to 21 defects per day.

59
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:23,480
Now, if we can reduce that by 25%, we will eliminate five defects per day.

60
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:29,600
Now, we obviously can't focus on all these defects, so the real question is: how do we know what to focus on?

61
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:31,940
And that's where the Pareto chart comes into play.

62
00:05:32,180 --> 00:05:39,980
So, the Pareto chart is another QC tool that allows you to analyze your data in search of the Pareto principle.

63
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:41,380
What is the Pareto rule?

64
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:43,020
What is this 80/20 rule?

65
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:55,540
This is a natural phenomenon discovered by a guy named Vilfredo Pareto, an Italian researcher studying land ownership and wealth distribution in Italy and Europe.

66
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:00,460
He found that 80% of the land was owned by 20% of the people.

67
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:06,520
And this 80-20 rule and this 80-20 phenomenon was also experienced by a guy named Joseph Juran.

68
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:14,360
Now, he gave credit for the tool to Vilfredo Pareto, but he was the one who popularized this idea of the 80-20 rule and this idea of the Pareto chart.

69
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:21,480
And what he told us and what he taught us is that a Pareto chart helps you separate the vital few from the trivial many.

70
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:22,680
Now, what did Juran mean?

71
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,440
What he means is when you're solving a problem, there's often one or two.

72
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:35,020
Key issues, key root causes or key defects that you need to focus on to have a major impact on that particular situation.

73
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:39,940
And that's exactly what you see here when we take our data from the check sheet and we put it into this Pareto chart.

74
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:46,500
We see that control PCB issues accounts for nearly forty percent of our defects.

75
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:51,660
You can see if we come across here, we've got forty percent of our defects coming directly from control PCB.

76
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:53,580
Now there's two things happening on this graph.

77
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:59,620
Obviously, there's the blue bars which are simply just the frequency or the count of defects that occurred throughout the week.

78
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:04,880
And then this black bar is actually the cumulative line, so this first defect accounts for 40 percent.

79
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:06,920
And then we go up and up and up all the way to 100 percent.

80
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:12,400
Now that we have this Pareto analysis, we know that control PCB is our primary issue.

81
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:14,560
It tells us what to focus on now.

82
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:21,240
We still don't understand why these issues are happening, and this is where something like the cause and effect diagram can be incredibly useful.

83
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:24,840
So this is the fishbone diagram or the Ishikawa diagram.

84
00:07:24,980 --> 00:07:28,220
There's all sorts of different names for it, but it is a cause and effect diagram.

85
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:33,580
And the way this works is we start with the effect that's over here on the right, that's the head of the fish here in orange.

86
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:34,580
This is our effect.

87
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:40,380
And so step one of the cause and effect diagram is to start with a really well written problem statement.

88
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:47,180
So I put in PCB failures, but in reality you want to have a much more descriptive problem statement than this.

89
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:52,100
And once you have this effect, you can start working through the fishbone process.

90
00:07:52,420 --> 00:07:55,700
To analyze all of the potential causes and failures.

91
00:07:55,780 --> 00:08:03,900
Now, I'm showing here what's called the eight M's, and this is the beauty of the fishbone process is that it's a well structured approach to root cause analysis.

92
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:12,820
It forces you to think about all of the potential different categories or scenarios or causes that might be contributing to your problem.

93
00:08:13,460 --> 00:08:17,620
Now, along with the cause and effect diagram are a number of tools that you should be using.

94
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:28,440
I would recommend you get out your flow chart, look at your process, use your flow chart and ask yourself how might each step in the process fail and contribute to the effect that we're seeing.

95
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:30,520
Teamwork is also a must here.

96
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:44,840
You're not going to be a subject matter expert in all of those eight M's and you need people from operations and engineering and quality and R&D and marketing and maintenance to really do a thorough analysis in each of those areas to truly understand the root cause.

97
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:50,080
And then of course brainstorming, you know, you're going to have to creatively think about and talk about and discuss.

98
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:54,640
Potential root causes that maybe you're not even aware of, and then the 5Y analysis.

99
00:08:54,800 --> 00:09:04,600
I love the 5Ys; it really helps you go from a high-level symptom down to the true root cause and really ask why, why, why to truly get to those real root causes that you need to address.

100
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:14,000
And then as you have that team discussion and you go through the process, you can identify potential root causes and contributing factors to the problem you're trying to solve.

101
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:21,040
Now obviously again, we have to go back to that pretty principle: we can't focus on everything; we have to talk about the most.

102
00:09:21,080 --> 00:09:24,240
likely root causes and the most likely contributing factors.

103
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:30,040
So again, at the end of your cause and effect diagram, you might identify three or four issues that you need to study further.

104
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:33,000
Now I want to talk about this one: high humidity during assembly.

105
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:41,600
Now as we were working through the cause and effect diagram process, the engineer who was helping us looked at our check sheet and noticed an interesting pattern.

106
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:46,720
What they noticed here, and I've highlighted it here in yellow, Is it Sunday, Monday, Tuesday?

107
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,760
We only had a few defects, right?

108
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:50,280
Six and four and one.

109
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:54,520
Whereas on Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, you'll notice that our defect rate jumped up a little bit.

110
00:09:54,920 --> 00:10:02,040
And what the engineer remembered is that we had a rainstorm come through on Tuesday night, and the humidity level in the facility really jumped up.

111
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:07,040
And so what the hypothesis here is that humidity is affecting our defect rate.

112
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:12,120
So I created this little table here to show the days of the week along with the defects.

113
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:18,140
And the humidity now to truly understand this relationship, we have to create a scatter diagram.

114
00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:21,780
So here's exactly what that scatter diagram looks like.

115
00:10:22,260 --> 00:10:24,540
What we do here is we're plotting pairs of data.

116
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:28,860
So for example, on Sunday, we had six defects and 18% humidity.

117
00:10:29,540 --> 00:10:30,500
You can see that right here.

118
00:10:30,540 --> 00:10:31,460
That's this data point right here.

119
00:10:31,540 --> 00:10:33,820
We had six defects, 18% humidity.

120
00:10:34,420 --> 00:10:38,780
Now the way this scatter diagram works, or you might hear this called an XY scatter plot.

121
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:46,120
Here on the X axis, we put our controllable variable, our independent variable, and then on the Y axis, we put our response variable.

122
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:54,400
So here we believe that relative humidity is the independent variable that is affecting our response variable, which is defects.

123
00:10:54,720 --> 00:11:01,240
And you can see here that there appears to be some relationship between PCB failures and humidity.

124
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:07,880
Now it's really important when you're looking at a scatter diagram not to assume that this relationship is a causal relationship.

125
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:09,560
There's this really important concept.

126
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:12,840
That you can have correlation without causation.

127
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:17,400
Two parameters or two variables can correlate without having a cause and effect relationship.

128
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:25,800
So let's assume though, let's assume that we've done a DOE here and we've proven that humidity has an effect on our PCB defects.

129
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:28,000
We could come back to the scatter diagram.

130
00:11:28,280 --> 00:11:32,960
We could say, okay, our target for PCB defects is five or less.

131
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:34,880
Let's call it, let's call it five or less.

132
00:11:34,920 --> 00:11:42,840
And so we come down here to humidity and say, okay, we want to control humidity to around 20% to keep our defects low.

133
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:43,800
Does that make sense?

134
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:50,360
And this is a great way; a scatter diagram is a great way to understand the relationship between two possible variables.

135
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:55,600
Now, once you've done your scatter diagram, you can quantify the relationship between those two variables.

136
00:11:55,920 --> 00:11:58,920
So what I'm showing here is the Pearson correlation coefficient.

137
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:05,240
And this coefficient ranges from positive one all the way over here on the left to negative one all the way over here on the right.

138
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:11,560
And that ranges from a perfectly positive correlation here, you can see that as x changes, y changes identically.

139
00:12:12,040 --> 00:12:15,600
And then same thing here with r equals minus one, this is a perfect negative correlation.

140
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:19,760
Now, as we get closer to zero, we start to lose that relationship.

141
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:24,840
So an R value of zero means there's no correlation between those two parameters.

142
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,040
As X changes, Y basically does whatever it wants.

143
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:29,880
There's no relationship between those two variables.

144
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:35,600
Now the next thing we could do in our analysis is to look at relative humidity over time.

145
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:52,840
So let's say we go out, we talk to our facilities engineers, we say okay, give us the relative humidity within our environment every six hours for the last six months and we can take that data and we want to plot it because we need to understand how relative humidity is changing within our facility.

146
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,960
One of the ways you could analyze that data is with a histogram.

147
00:12:56,360 --> 00:13:02,560
So, a histogram is just a very simple bar chart that graphs the frequency of occurrence of continuous data.

148
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:06,160
And again, this is a great way to talk about your process.

149
00:13:06,200 --> 00:13:14,920
Every process or every product or every quality attribute out there has some level of random normal variation that will often occur in a pattern.

150
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:21,320
And as engineers, we need to understand what is the pattern associated with our outputs or our process.

151
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:26,800
And a histogram is a great way to understand the pattern or the variation in your process.

152
00:13:27,220 --> 00:13:57,180
Now you might grab this data and you might get like a skewed distribution or maybe a bimodal distribution or an exponential distribution there's all sorts of distributions you might get but it's great to know how your process is behaving now the other beautiful part about a histogram is you can take this data and let's overlay some specification limits right now what we have is the beginnings of process capability so the histogram is a fantastic tool to quantify and understand how your process behaves.

153
00:13:57,260 --> 00:14:02,020
And if you compare that against the specification limits, we can now start talking about process capability.

154
00:14:03,100 --> 00:14:05,700
Okay, so we're on to the very last and final QC tool.

155
00:14:06,140 --> 00:14:12,980
Let's assume we now control for humidity and we want to make sure that that change has been effective over time.

156
00:14:13,020 --> 00:14:16,460
A control chart is the right tool or the perfect tool to do that.

157
00:14:16,500 --> 00:14:17,620
So what is a control chart?

158
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:23,100
It is essentially a tool that allows you to confirm that your process is in control.

159
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:28,280
Now when I say in control, what I mean is that you're only experiencing normal variation.

160
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:35,320
When your process is experiencing normal cause variation, your data should fall within those control limits.

161
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:39,480
By the way, if you're new to SPC, I have a whole separate video on control charts.

162
00:14:39,560 --> 00:14:40,280
You can go check it out.

163
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:43,400
I've got both the X-bar and R chart as well as attribute data.

164
00:14:43,440 --> 00:14:50,400
And a control chart is a fantastic tool to use at the end of a project to monitor and control your process and make sure that your changes were effective.

165
00:14:50,980 --> 00:14:54,500
And let's take a look at what this looks like for our particular process.

166
00:14:54,820 --> 00:14:55,860
So here's our process, right?

167
00:14:55,980 --> 00:15:02,580
The first week of data, you can see we're really all over the place and our control limits are really wide because we're not controlling for humidity.

168
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:08,380
And we've got all this data and you can see on average, we have about eight defects per day, right?

169
00:15:08,420 --> 00:15:09,740
And we're really jumping around here.

170
00:15:09,780 --> 00:15:12,940
And then let's say on day nine, we start controlling for relative humidity.

171
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:16,140
And we've got our control chart and we're collecting data.

172
00:15:16,260 --> 00:15:21,940
You can see that for the next 20-plus days, our defect rate has dramatically fallen.

173
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:26,180
In fact, our new mean defects per day is around three.

174
00:15:26,620 --> 00:15:35,560
So essentially, we've gone from eight defects per day down to three defects per day, and we've hit our target of reducing defects by 25%.

175
00:15:36,020 --> 00:15:41,980
We've gone from 20-plus defects a day down to about 15, all by controlling relative humidity in our process.

176
